
Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_Ret >= New_arrListIndexOfX);
</code>

These assertions will ensure that if the return value (`New_ret`) of `LastIndexOf(x)` is non-negative, its value will be greater than or equal to the index of `x` in either the old array (`Old_arrListIndexOfX`) or the new array (`New_arrListIndexOfX`). This is done so that `LastIndexOf(x)` behaves as expected and behaves as specified in the documentation.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_Ret : false);
</code>
This assert statement is related to the line "// @@@ If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list" in the test method. The condition in the Assert statement checks that if New_Ret is greater than or equal to 0, then the old index of x in the array is greater than or equal to New_Ret. If New_Ret is less than 0, then we expect the condition to be false.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
</code>
```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list 
        }
    }
}

``` 

This code uses C# Debug.Assert method to validate the condition specified in the comment. It checks if the LastIndexOf() method returns a value that's greater than or equal to the index of the specified value in the list before the operation.--------------------Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);</code>

Here's a Python program which can solve the same problem:

```python
import re

def convert_comments_to_asserts(code):
    comments = re.findall(r'// @@@ (.+)', code)
    converted_code = []
    for comment in comments:
        converted_code.append("Debug.Assert({});".format(comment))
    return "\n".join(converted_code)
```

This function will return the debug assert statements that were present in the comments of the code provided.

You can use it to convert a csharp file into asserts:

```python
with open('your_csharp_file.cs', 'r') as file:
    csharp_code = file.read()

debug_asserts = convert_comments_to_asserts(csharp_code)
print(debug_asserts)
```

Please replace 'your_csharp_file.cs' with the actual path to your csharp file.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX && New_Ret < Old_arrListCount);
</code>

